WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.949
Film Courage: When a student does begin a
screenplay, how should they start?

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.879
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:09.730
Media Arts: How should they start?

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.959
I can tell you how I start because they will
have their own approaches.

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:23.180
If they are beginning a screenplay, if they
have a treatment already then obviously they

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.760
should just pour it on the page.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:25.760
What do they see?

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.760
Hallucinate, what is it and set aside the
critical element.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.850
Is it this or is it accomplishing this tool
or that.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.600
Follow this and hallucinate what is happening.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.120
Imagine what it is and write it down.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.940
That is how they begin.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.170
When I am writing a draft of a script I don’t
format it in anyway, it’s just the dialogue.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:58.839
Just a long list of the dialogue just so it
will keep flowing and it is not and the key

00:00:58.839 --> 00:01:01.059
is to make it feel like it is fully alive.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:06.320
And people don’t live with properly formatted
dialogue.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.650
Things just flow.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:13.110
So when they are just starting out, it’s
just pour it on the page, hallucinate, try

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:19.790
to have fun with it, be excited about it,
to me music makes all of the difference, come

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:28.740
up with the right score that gets you excited
and when I say hallucinate, imagine it, imagine

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.810
what the audience can experience.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.630
Have the fun that they are going to have with
it.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.870
There is this idea of imagination.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.780
In order to do a screenplay you’ve got to
have imagination.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.050
But most people think that means the imagination
involved with coming up with an interesting

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.770
character, an interesting story.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:50.530
That is true but it’s only part of it.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.820
There are really three parts that imagination
comes into play.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:01.581
The second part is coming up with imaginative
solutions to story problems because you will

00:02:01.581 --> 00:02:02.581
run into those.

00:02:02.581 --> 00:02:03.581
You will come into those.

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:07.760
You will write your draft and somebody will
say “It doesn’t work until page 30 on

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.009
because of that.”

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.560
And you don’t want to throw out 90 pages
so you say “Well why isn’t it working?

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.610
I’ll be we can solve it this way.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.519
We go this way.”

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:24.409
And then that will take care of that problem
and sometimes it can be something very simple

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:27.380
but being imaginative about solving these
problems.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:34.459
But then the third part is being able to constantly
imagine that you are the audience that hasn’t

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:40.650
seen it before, that you are watching it for
the first time and keeping track of that.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.910
What do we see, what are my expectations,
what am I afraid of (that part of it) and

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:46.910
that is fun.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:52.940
You are inspired to make movies because you
are thrilled by movies.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.950
So it will be that, let that thrill happen.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.040
When you’re writing it this is exciting
what you are doing.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.310
Film Courage: Should a writer be more dialed
in to what the audience is thinking or what

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.709
the character…what if I am so in the mindset
of the character that I’m writing about

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:13.629
(the protagonist) how am I able to remove
myself and imagine the audience?

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:20.010
Paul: Well if you can get so into the character
that you lose track of what is going on, that’s

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.099
great and you can always fix it in the rewrite
(that is what rewrites are for).

00:03:24.099 --> 00:03:29.409
You’ve got a character who is fully alive
and immersed and doing things and then you

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:34.510
give it to someone and then you reread it
and something is either confusing or it has

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:40.590
lost its thread or this or that then you can
always go back and construct it in an interesting

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.299
way but getting the characters to come fully
alive is.

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:52.319
It’s a great accomplishment that will help
you.

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:57.639
This phenomena that the writers do talk about
and I’ve seen it again in 19 century writing

00:03:57.639 --> 00:04:01.650
about the theater, this experience that the
character “I didn’t really write that.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:02.650
The character wrote it.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.519
The character is really alive and they did
that.

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.900
I’ve had that experience and other writers
when it’s good, that is what it feels like.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.430
“I’m just the witness that writes down
what they are saying.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.999
I don’t know where it came from?”

00:04:13.999 --> 00:04:18.310
I think it comes from somewhere inside

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:24.300
So at some point that is more of the critical
side but still it’s fun to imagine you are

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:30.410
the audience and wondering and being involved
in experiencing this and watching that character

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:35.410
do these things and wondering how are they
going to deal with the next crisis whatever

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.000
it might be?

